<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Horace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">65–8 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Quintus Horatius Flaccus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Horace, Quintus Horatius Flaccus, was a leading Roman poet of the age of Augustus. The son of a freedman from Venusia, he fought for Brutus at Philippi and later won the patronage of Maecenas. His Odes, Satires, Epistles and Ars Poetica, with their wit and moral reflection, were among the most widely read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poetry in later European literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horace.jpg" alt="Horace" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(contorniate_M%C3%A9daillon_Horace_Rome_(...)Horace_(0065-0008_btv1b11343890r_1_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Horace was born on 8 December 65 BCE at Venusia, a town on a trade route in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a> on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. Various Italic dialects were spoken in the area, which perhaps enriched his feeling for language. He may have thought of himself as a Samnite by birth, and references to his childhood setting run through his poems.</p><p>He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, possibly after his father's death, and continued his education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, arriving at nineteen. The city was then dominated by Epicureans and Stoics, whose theories made a deep impression on him. There he probably acquired a deep familiarity with Greek lyric poetry. He served as an officer in the republican army defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-philippi">Battle of Philippi</a> in 42 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His career coincided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s change from republic to empire. He was befriended by Octavian's right-hand man in civil affairs, Maecenas, and became a spokesman for the new regime. Some commentators see a fine balance in which he kept a strong measure of independence, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-dryden">John Dryden</a> called him &quot;a well-mannered court slave&quot;. The dating of his works is only approximate: the Satires belong to about 35 to 30 BCE, the Epodes to 30 BCE and Odes 1 to 3 to about 23 BCE, followed by the Epistles, the Carmen Saeculare of 17 BCE, Odes 4 and the Ars Poetica.</p><p>He composed in metres borrowed from archaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, hexameters for the Satires and Epistles, iambs for the Epodes and alcaics and sapphics for the Odes. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> include politics, love, philosophy, ethics, his own social role and poetry itself. In the Odes Epicureanism is the dominant influence, and in Odes 3.30 he claimed to have built a monument more durable than bronze.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Horace died on 27 November 8 BCE. Odes 1 to 3 were at first not well received, but Augustus commissioned a ceremonial ode for the Centennial Games in 17 BCE and encouraged the publication of Odes 4, after which his standing as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s premier lyricist was assured. Quintilian regarded the Odes as the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> lyrics worth reading, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> and Propertius were among those influenced by him.</p><p>His poems probably survived the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> in just two or three books, ancestors of six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the ninth century. In the 17th and 18th centuries new editions appeared almost yearly and ode-writing became fashionable in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He remained central to the education of English-speaking elites until the 1960s. For Wilfred Owen, marked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, his line dulce et decorum est stood for discredited values.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Horace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Virgil’s Aeneid gave Rome a national epic, <b>Horace</b> wrote odes and satires, Ovid retold the myths of transformation in the Metamorphoses, and Livy wrote a history of Rome from its foundation.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>…1784 Immanuel Kant defined enlightenment as “man’s emergence from his self-incurred immaturity”, the inability to use one’s own understanding without another’s guidance, and gave it a motto from the Roman poet <b>Horace</b>, “Sapere aude”, dare to know, which he rendered as “Have courage to use your own understanding!”</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>“Captive Greece took captive her savage conqueror,” wrote the poet <b>Horace</b>, “and brought the arts into rustic Latium.”</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Let My People Go: The Song of the Contrabands&quot;, arranged by <b>Horace</b> Waters, the first spiritual known to have been published as sheet music.</blockquote><a href="/songs/go-down-moses">Go Down Moses</a></li><li><blockquote>Whether they composed the tune is disputed: Kembrew McLeod argues that they likely took it from similar 19th-century songs such as <b>Horace</b> Waters' &quot;Happy Greetings to All&quot; (1858), while Robert Brauneis notes that those songs had quite different melodies.</blockquote><a href="/songs/happy-birthday-to-you">Happy Birthday to You</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years' War, the Imperial-Spanish army of Johann Tserclaes, Count of Tilly, took Mannheim from Protestant troops under the Englishman Sir <b>Horace</b> Vere.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-2">2 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Venosa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.97,15.82&amp;year=-28" aria-label="Venosa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4937 701 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Horace"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5387 971h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5387 971h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.97,15.82&amp;year=-28">What was here in 28 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In the moment of our talking, envious time has ebb'd away. Seize the present; trust to-morrow e'en as little as you may.&#8221;</p><footer>Odes, book 1, ode 11 &#183; tr. John Conington, 1863 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5432/pg5432-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And now 'tis done: more durable than brass My monument shall be, and raise its head O'er royal pyramids: it shall not dread Corroding rain or angry Boreas, Nor the long lapse of immemorial time.&#8221;</p><footer>Odes, book 3, ode 30 &#183; tr. John Conington, 1863 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5432/pg5432-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Greece, conquered Greece, her conqueror subdued, And Rome grew polished, who till then was rude…&#8221;</p><footer>Epistles, book 2, letter 1 &#183; tr. John Conington, 1869 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5419/pg5419-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What joy, for fatherland to die! Death's darts e'en flying feet o'ertake, Nor spare a recreant chivalry, A back that cowers, or loins that quake.&#8221;</p><footer>Odes, book 3, ode 2 &#183; tr. John Conington, 1863 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5432/pg5432-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Pale Death, impartial, walks his round; he knocks at cottage-gate And palace-portal.&#8221;</p><footer>Odes, book 1, ode 4 &#183; tr. John Conington, 1863 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5432/pg5432-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Come now, have courage to be wise: begin: You're halfway over when you once plunge in…&#8221;</p><footer>Epistles, book 1, letter 2 &#183; tr. John Conington, 1869 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5419/pg5419-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
